course: dictionary
lesson: 10412
title: A trillion and trillions
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A trillion is a very big number: 1,000,000,000,000, which is a 1 followed by 12 zeros. Count the steps: a thousand has 3 zeros, a million has 6, a billion has 9, and a trillion has 12. Each step is a thousand times bigger than the one before.
explain: A trillion is also a million millions. You can write it as 10 to the power of 12, which means twelve 10s multiplied together. Scientists use this kind of shortcut when numbers get too long to write out.
explain: Now look at the word in context. «Your gut holds trillions of useful bacteria.» Here trillions does not mean exactly one trillion. It means an enormous number, too big to count. Check by swapping in your meaning: «Your gut holds an enormous number of useful bacteria» still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: A trillion is 1 followed by 12 zeros: 1,000,000,000,000.
fact: A trillion is a million millions.
fact: Place value grows by a thousand each step: thousand, million, billion, trillion.
fact: «Trillions» can mean an enormous, uncountable number, as in «I've told you trillions of times!»
fact: «Red dwarfs can last trillions of years», so trillions can also describe very long periods of time.
exercise: How many zeros does a trillion have? | 9 | 3 | *12 | 6
exercise: «I've told you trillions of times!» What does trillions mean here? | Exactly one trillion | A small number of times | *An enormous, uncountable number | A type of bacteria
exercise: Which number is a million millions? | 1,000,000,000 | *1,000,000,000,000 | 1,000,000,000,000,000 | 1,000,000
test: Which number is a trillion? | 1,000 | 1,000,000,000 | 1,000,000 | *1,000,000,000,000
test: Which place-value word has 9 zeros? | Million | Trillion | Thousand | *Billion
exam: Each place-value word from thousand to trillion is how many times bigger than the one before? | 10 times | *1,000 times | 100 times | 2 times
